WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.942
This is the executive
briefing for Book 04 of the

3
00:00:03.942 --> 00:00:07.600
Federal Modernization
Workbook, Access and Privilege.

4
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.087
It's for the people who
make the calls on access.

5
00:00:10.087 --> 00:00:11.513
The argument is short.

6
00:00:11.513 --> 00:00:15.555
Under Zero Trust, every access
request is decided by policy,

7
00:00:15.555 --> 00:00:19.762
so your Conditional Access policy
set is your access control.

8
00:00:19.762 --> 00:00:23.766
Standing administrator rights
turn one stolen credential into

9
00:00:23.766 --> 00:00:27.400
control of the tenant, and a
stronger sign-in doesn't fix that.

10
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.813
And the platform won't separate
deciding from doing for you.

11
00:00:30.813 --> 00:00:34.049
I'll take four questions:
why, what, who and how.

12
00:00:34.049 --> 00:00:38.425
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner and when it's needed.

13
00:00:38.425 --> 00:00:41.200
Let's start with how access gets decided.

14
00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:43.988
Access used to be settled once.

15
00:00:43.988 --> 00:00:46.942
You joined a group, your
desk sat inside the network,

16
00:00:46.942 --> 00:00:50.037
and every request after that
was approved by default.

17
00:00:50.037 --> 00:00:52.912
Federal policy doesn't
accept that any more.

18
00:00:52.912 --> 00:00:56.626
M-22-09 says every request gets evaluated,

19
00:00:56.626 --> 00:00:59.350
and the network earns no trust on its own.

20
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:03.575
In Entra ID, the thing that
evaluates is Conditional Access.

21
00:01:03.575 --> 00:01:04.900
Its rules are simple.

22
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.500
Every policy that applies has to be met.

23
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.188
A block always wins.

24
00:01:09.188 --> 00:01:11.775
And an exclusion overrides everything.

25
00:01:11.775 --> 00:01:16.687
That last one is the dangerous rule,
because each exclusion is a standing hole.

26
00:01:16.687 --> 00:01:20.666
Only the emergency access
accounts are excluded by design;

27
00:01:20.666 --> 00:01:24.238
everything else needs an
owner, a reason and an expiry.

28
00:01:24.238 --> 00:01:27.100
Next, why the old habits survive.

29
00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.763
Two habits survive almost every migration.

30
00:01:30.763 --> 00:01:32.962
Inside the network means trusted.

31
00:01:32.962 --> 00:01:35.449
And in the group means allowed, forever.

32
00:01:35.449 --> 00:01:39.287
Policy replaces both, but it
leans hard on directory data.

33
00:01:39.287 --> 00:01:43.453
The program targets policies at
groups built from attributes,

34
00:01:43.453 --> 00:01:47.538
the way Group Policy once targeted
organizational units (OUs).

35
00:01:47.538 --> 00:01:51.896
So a user with an empty attribute
simply falls outside the policy:

36
00:01:51.896 --> 00:01:53.913
an exclusion nobody approved.

37
00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:56.435
And group changes can
take more than a day,

38
00:01:56.435 --> 00:02:00.738
so urgent removals mean disabling
the account and revoking sessions.

39
00:02:00.738 --> 00:02:06.378
The decision for the CISO: baseline policies
cover all users and exclude narrowly,

40
00:02:06.378 --> 00:02:11.038
and no attribute-based policy is
enforced until its data is verified.

41
00:02:11.038 --> 00:02:12.900
HR owns those attributes.

42
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.120
Next, administrators.

43
00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.520
Here's where it matters most.

44
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.158
A Global Administrator can rewrite the
policies that protect everyone else.

45
00:02:22.158 --> 00:02:28.183
If that role is always on, it's
exposed all 8,760 hours of the year.

46
00:02:28.183 --> 00:02:31.698
NSA and CISA rank weak
separation of admin privilege

47
00:02:31.698 --> 00:02:34.745
second among the
misconfigurations they find.

48
00:02:34.745 --> 00:02:38.336
And it's not only people; Midnight
Blizzard used a forgotten

49
00:02:38.336 --> 00:02:41.633
application with standing
access to reach mailboxes.

50
00:02:41.633 --> 00:02:43.932
A FIDO2 key proves who you are.

51
00:02:43.932 --> 00:02:46.858
It doesn't limit what you
can do, or for how long.

52
00:02:46.858 --> 00:02:49.517
So the decision: every human admin role is

53
00:02:49.517 --> 00:02:52.645
eligible and activated
just in time through PIM.

54
00:02:52.645 --> 00:02:54.119
The CISO sets that model,

55
00:02:54.119 --> 00:02:58.120
and the authorizing official accepts
whatever has to stay standing.

56
00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.210
Next, what the rules ask.

57
00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.138
NIST's AC-6 asks for only the access
a task needs, with privileges reviewed

58
00:03:06.138 --> 00:03:11.747
on a schedule and every privileged action
logged, all in the Moderate baseline.

59
00:03:11.747 --> 00:03:15.950
M-22-09 doesn't actually
mandate time-bound privilege,

60
00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:17.936
and it's often quoted as if it did.

61
00:03:17.936 --> 00:03:22.708
CISA's maturity model is the clearest:
it runs from permanent access

62
00:03:22.708 --> 00:03:27.398
with a periodic review, to automated
just-in-time, just-enough access.

63
00:03:27.398 --> 00:03:31.460
Most estates with standing administrators
are still at the first stage.

64
00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:36.098
Microsoft's numbers are short: fewer
than five Global Administrators.

65
00:03:36.098 --> 00:03:40.461
The takeaway is that time is the
dimension most estates never limited,

66
00:03:40.461 --> 00:03:44.673
and limiting it gives you the review
and log evidence as a by-product.

67
00:03:44.673 --> 00:03:46.950
Now, the baseline itself.

68
00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:53.303
The book's policy library is built to
CISA's Binding Operational Directive 25-01,

69
00:03:53.303 --> 00:03:58.487
which made a set of cloud configurations
mandatory for civilian agencies.

70
00:03:58.487 --> 00:04:03.363
Each required Entra item maps to a
named policy: block legacy sign-in,

71
00:04:03.363 --> 00:04:05.800
block high-risk users and sign-ins,

72
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:10.588
and phishing-resistant MFA for
everyone and for privileged roles.

73
00:04:10.588 --> 00:04:12.400
One choice needs your attention.

74
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:18.025
Microsoft recommends asking high-risk users
to fix their own risk; CISA says block.

75
00:04:18.025 --> 00:04:22.250
They can't both win, so in a
directive-scoped tenant the program blocks,

76
00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:24.400
and the help desk handles recovery.

77
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.613
The policies roll out in five gated waves,
each clean for a week before the next.

78
00:04:29.613 --> 00:04:31.479
The decision for the CISO:

79
00:04:31.479 --> 00:04:35.637
adopt the library and its waves
before wave one is enforced.

80
00:04:35.637 --> 00:04:37.650
Next, the end state.

81
00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.986
The end state is phishing-resistant
MFA for everyone,

82
00:04:41.986 --> 00:04:44.512
and the directive makes it mandatory.

83
00:04:44.512 --> 00:04:47.349
Ordinary MFA is allowed only until then,

84
00:04:47.349 --> 00:04:50.787
so every policy that
accepts it is transitional.

85
00:04:50.787 --> 00:04:55.879
The phishing-resistant methods are Windows
Hello, FIDO2 passkeys and certificates,

86
00:04:55.879 --> 00:04:59.626
and administrators get a stricter
version on a separate device.

87
00:04:59.626 --> 00:05:03.416
The end-state policy goes in on
day one in report-only mode,

88
00:05:03.416 --> 00:05:06.400
so the logs build a history
of who'd be blocked.

89
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.300
Then enforcement follows
measured readiness:

90
00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.538
the program's gates are 95 percent
of users registered and 90 percent

91
00:05:13.538 --> 00:05:17.850
of devices compliant, and every
not-ready exception carries an expiry.

92
00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.713
The CISO owns that schedule.

93
00:05:19.713 --> 00:05:23.290
Next, two platform behaviors
that surprise people.

94
00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:27.504
First, a policy that says
Finance must use strong

95
00:05:27.504 --> 00:05:31.228
MFA to reach payroll says
nothing about anyone else.

96
00:05:31.228 --> 00:05:34.702
If no policy applies, the
platform issues a token.

97
00:05:34.702 --> 00:05:36.870
So restricting an app takes a pair:

98
00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:41.591
one policy that grants with conditions,
and one that blocks everyone else.

99
00:05:41.591 --> 00:05:45.577
Second, Microsoft creates some
policies in your tenant itself.

100
00:05:45.577 --> 00:05:48.363
They arrive in report-only
mode and switch on at

101
00:05:48.363 --> 00:05:51.577
least thirty days later,
unless someone turns them off.

102
00:05:51.577 --> 00:05:53.865
That's a change the agency didn't make.

103
00:05:53.865 --> 00:05:57.625
So the decision: the identity
team records, for each one,

104
00:05:57.625 --> 00:06:01.551
whether to adopt it or supersede
it with a library policy,

105
00:06:01.551 --> 00:06:05.478
inside that thirty-day window,
under the CISO's baseline.

106
00:06:05.478 --> 00:06:07.950
Next, how privilege is tiered.

107
00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.850
Privileged roles are tiered by
what their holder could take over.

108
00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:17.371
Tier 0 is the control plane: Global
Administrator and the roles that grant roles,

109
00:06:17.371 --> 00:06:20.800
reset admin credentials
or change sign-in policy.

110
00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.700
Tier 1 runs a whole service,
like Exchange or Intune.

111
00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:30.788
Tier 2 works inside a bounded population,
scoped to administrative units (AUs).

112
00:06:30.788 --> 00:06:33.726
Conditional Access
Administrator is Tier 0,

113
00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:37.062
because it can weaken the
gate on elevation itself.

114
00:06:37.062 --> 00:06:42.076
Exchange Administrator isn't labeled
privileged, yet it can reach every mailbox.

115
00:06:42.076 --> 00:06:46.788
And readers are tiered, because the
security setup is a map for an attacker.

116
00:06:46.788 --> 00:06:50.804
The decision for the CISO:
approve each role's tier and gate,

117
00:06:50.804 --> 00:06:55.476
and keep Global Administrators under
five, before any role converts.

118
00:06:55.476 --> 00:06:57.090
Next, the gate.

119
00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:01.400
An eligible administrator asks
for the role, gives a reason,

120
00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:06.340
proves who they are again with a phishing-resistant
sign-in, and waits for approval.

121
00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:11.652
The role then lasts only the window:
four hours at Tier 0, eight at Tier 1.

122
00:07:11.652 --> 00:07:16.328
Eligibility itself expires too,
so it has to be re-authorized.

123
00:07:16.328 --> 00:07:18.241
The platform stops self-approval,

124
00:07:18.241 --> 00:07:22.478
but it won't stop you making the approvers
and the requesters the same group.

125
00:07:22.478 --> 00:07:25.570
So approvers sit outside
the tier they approve,

126
00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:28.028
and Tier 0's sit in the security office.

127
00:07:28.028 --> 00:07:30.828
And approvers have
twenty-four hours, fixed.

128
00:07:30.828 --> 00:07:35.837
The decision for the CISO: staff each
approver group from outside its tier,

129
00:07:35.837 --> 00:07:38.503
at least two people, sized for real hours.

130
00:07:38.503 --> 00:07:41.720
Next, what can quietly undo all this.

131
00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:45.895
A good gate can be undone
quietly, in four ways.

132
00:07:45.895 --> 00:07:47.901
If your premium licenses lapse,

133
00:07:47.901 --> 00:07:52.145
eligible roles are removed and
expiring grants become permanent.

134
00:07:52.145 --> 00:07:55.868
Whoever can edit Conditional
Access can weaken the gate,

135
00:07:55.868 --> 00:08:00.620
so those roles sit in Tier 0, and policy
changes need a fresh strong sign-in.

136
00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:04.575
If approval is on but nobody's
named, the default approvers are the

137
00:08:04.575 --> 00:08:08.970
very admins you made eligible, and
Microsoft documents that as a lockout.

138
00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:14.075
And Microsoft retires the older
PIM interface on October 28,

139
00:08:14.075 --> 00:08:16.832
2026, so old scripts simply break.

140
00:08:16.832 --> 00:08:18.883
The first one is a funding call.

141
00:08:18.883 --> 00:08:21.944
The sponsor and CIO
treat these licenses as

142
00:08:21.944 --> 00:08:25.007
a control dependency,
renewed without a gap.

143
00:08:25.007 --> 00:08:27.140
Next, the one exception.

144
00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:32.565
Every control that blocks an admin path
also creates a way to lock yourself out.

145
00:08:32.565 --> 00:08:37.953
So there's one standing exception:
emergency access, or break-glass, accounts.

146
00:08:37.953 --> 00:08:42.385
Two or more cloud-only accounts,
permanently Global Administrator,

147
00:08:42.385 --> 00:08:45.313
with phishing-resistant
sign-in of their own,

148
00:08:45.313 --> 00:08:49.428
excluded only from blocking
policies, and alerted on every use.

149
00:08:49.428 --> 00:08:53.302
Using them is strict: two
senior administrators together,

150
00:08:53.302 --> 00:08:57.823
only when PIM is down and the problem
can't wait, out within four hours,

151
00:08:57.823 --> 00:09:01.214
reviewed within a day, and
tested every ninety days.

152
00:09:01.214 --> 00:09:02.440
Two decisions.

153
00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:05.540
Build and test them before
any role goes eligible.

154
00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:07.895
And be honest in the security plan:

155
00:09:07.895 --> 00:09:13.027
NIST expects emergency accounts to
expire, so record these as a deviation.

156
00:09:13.027 --> 00:09:16.603
The CISO builds; the
authorizing official accepts.

157
00:09:16.603 --> 00:09:18.350
Now, who decides.

158
00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.562
The principle is that
deciding isn't doing.

159
00:09:21.562 --> 00:09:25.759
Approvers don't make changes, the
people who make changes don't approve,

160
00:09:25.759 --> 00:09:28.001
and nobody approves their own access.

161
00:09:28.001 --> 00:09:31.225
Here's the catch: Entra
doesn't enforce any of that.

162
00:09:31.225 --> 00:09:34.102
Application Administrator can grant most

163
00:09:34.102 --> 00:09:36.900
kinds of consent and
can even act as any app.

164
00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:39.081
So if the help desk holds that role,

165
00:09:39.081 --> 00:09:42.975
the rule that it never approves
consent exists only on paper.

166
00:09:42.975 --> 00:09:44.312
The fix is design.

167
00:09:44.312 --> 00:09:47.457
Every request is triaged
by the authority it needs,

168
00:09:47.457 --> 00:09:52.550
and the help desk gets a custom role that
manages app assignments and nothing else.

169
00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:55.459
The decision for the
identity governance officer:

170
00:09:55.459 --> 00:09:59.337
narrow that role before the help
desk takes its first access ticket.

171
00:09:59.337 --> 00:10:01.610
Next, how apps are tiered.

172
00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:04.960
For applications, risk sets the chain.

173
00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:10.160
Every app gets a risk tier from the identity
governance officer before anyone gets

174
00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:13.626
access, and one high-risk trait
is enough to make it Tier 1:

175
00:10:13.626 --> 00:10:18.298
an application permission, sensitive
data, an outside operator, or no owner.

176
00:10:18.298 --> 00:10:20.999
The tier then decides who approves access,

177
00:10:20.999 --> 00:10:24.085
who approves consent, and
how often it's reviewed.

178
00:10:24.085 --> 00:10:26.838
Consent is the riskiest
request you'll see,

179
00:10:26.838 --> 00:10:31.085
because a grant covers the whole
tenant at once and never expires.

180
00:10:31.085 --> 00:10:34.112
So any application permission
gets Tier 1 handling,

181
00:10:34.112 --> 00:10:38.335
and critical scopes need the governance
officer and the CISO together.

182
00:10:38.335 --> 00:10:41.929
The decision: tier every
app before the first grant,

183
00:10:41.929 --> 00:10:45.523
and treat anything untiered
or ownerless as Tier 1.

184
00:10:45.523 --> 00:10:48.070
Next, apps as identities.

185
00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:53.095
Applications need the same lifecycle
people get, including an end.

186
00:10:53.095 --> 00:10:54.420
Three facts drive it.

187
00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.271
Consent grants don't expire,

188
00:10:56.271 --> 00:11:00.133
and revoking one doesn't cancel
the tokens already issued.

189
00:11:00.133 --> 00:11:04.767
Owners aren't just contacts: an owner
can add a credential and become the app,

190
00:11:04.767 --> 00:11:07.533
so the list holds two
named technical people.

191
00:11:07.533 --> 00:11:12.134
And stored secrets are the weak spot,
so the order is a managed identity,

192
00:11:12.134 --> 00:11:15.307
then federation, then a
short-lived certificate.

193
00:11:15.307 --> 00:11:21.245
Microsoft's recommended policy blocks new
secrets and caps certificates at 180 days,

194
00:11:21.245 --> 00:11:24.171
and the program adopts
it for every new app.

195
00:11:24.171 --> 00:11:27.101
The CISO sets that rule,
and any exception for a

196
00:11:27.101 --> 00:11:30.258
secret needs the governance
officer and the CISO.

197
00:11:30.258 --> 00:11:33.250
Next, the whole map of who decides.

198
00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:35.325
One decider per row.

199
00:11:35.325 --> 00:11:39.088
The CISO sets the access baseline
and the privilege model.

200
00:11:39.088 --> 00:11:42.434
The identity governance
officer tiers applications,

201
00:11:42.434 --> 00:11:46.301
decides consent above the lowest
tier, and rules on exceptions.

202
00:11:46.301 --> 00:11:49.475
Application owners decide
who gets into their apps.

203
00:11:49.475 --> 00:11:51.638
Business approvers confirm the need.

204
00:11:51.638 --> 00:11:55.088
Security operations can pull
consent during an incident.

205
00:11:55.088 --> 00:11:57.187
The sponsor and CIO fund it.

206
00:11:57.187 --> 00:12:00.300
And the authorizing official
owns residual risk.

207
00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:05.048
Exceptions run on a clock: six months
at most, never renewed automatically,

208
00:12:05.048 --> 00:12:10.263
and one renewed more than twice goes up
to the CISO and the authorizing official.

209
00:12:10.263 --> 00:12:13.629
The decision: any exception
that leaves a control unmet

210
00:12:13.629 --> 00:12:17.362
with nothing compensating goes
to the authorizing official.

211
00:12:17.362 --> 00:12:18.940
Now, how to run it.

212
00:12:19.690 --> 00:12:22.306
Policy decides whether
a session goes ahead,

213
00:12:22.306 --> 00:12:25.628
not whether someone should
still hold last year's access.

214
00:12:25.628 --> 00:12:26.790
That's governance.

215
00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:29.474
People request access through packages,

216
00:12:29.474 --> 00:12:34.527
with named approvers and an end date from
day one; the program never uses never.

217
00:12:34.527 --> 00:12:38.712
Reviews run on a calendar set by
risk: monthly for Tier 0 roles,

218
00:12:38.712 --> 00:12:43.064
quarterly for the rest, and guests
quarterly by their sponsors.

219
00:12:43.064 --> 00:12:46.890
A mover loses the old access,
and a leaver loses it all.

220
00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:49.736
One trap: reviewing an
app doesn't remove the

221
00:12:49.736 --> 00:12:52.502
members of its groups,
so review the groups.

222
00:12:52.502 --> 00:12:54.677
The executive call is licensing.

223
00:12:54.677 --> 00:12:58.561
Partial licensing governs some
people and leaves the rest alone,

224
00:12:58.561 --> 00:13:02.516
so the sponsor and CIO license
governance for the whole workforce.

225
00:13:02.516 --> 00:13:04.240
Next, evidence.

226
00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:07.140
Finally, how decisions are held.

227
00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:11.090
Every policy and setting lives
as a file in a repository.

228
00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:14.790
A change is a reviewed request,
and its author can't approve it.

229
00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:18.215
New policies run in report-only
for at least a week.

230
00:13:18.215 --> 00:13:22.577
The pipeline has no stored secret,
and it's treated as Tier 0.

231
00:13:22.577 --> 00:13:27.273
Emergency edits in the portal are allowed,
but they're back in code within a day,

232
00:13:27.273 --> 00:13:31.602
and a monitor checks the tenant
against the repository every six hours.

233
00:13:31.602 --> 00:13:33.065
Then keep the evidence.

234
00:13:33.065 --> 00:13:35.923
Entra holds its logs for only thirty days,

235
00:13:35.923 --> 00:13:40.377
so the program keeps a year, and
three for consent and break-glass.

236
00:13:40.377 --> 00:13:42.169
The decision for the CISO:

237
00:13:42.169 --> 00:13:46.403
make the repository the only normal
path, from the first policy.

238
00:13:46.403 --> 00:13:48.030
Let's pull it together.

239
00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:50.355
So, the decisions.

240
00:13:50.355 --> 00:13:53.590
The CISO sets the baseline:
all users covered,

241
00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:59.549
attribute-based policies only on verified
data, the library adopted before wave one,

242
00:13:59.549 --> 00:14:03.380
and phishing-resistant MFA
in report-only from day one.

243
00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:08.456
The CISO also owns the privilege model:
every admin role eligible, tiers approved,

244
00:14:08.456 --> 00:14:12.861
approvers from outside the tier, and
break-glass built and tested first,

245
00:14:12.861 --> 00:14:16.743
with the authorizing official
accepting what stays standing.

246
00:14:16.743 --> 00:14:20.655
The sponsor and CIO fund
the licenses, without a gap.

247
00:14:20.655 --> 00:14:25.655
The identity governance officer narrows
the help desk role and tiers every app.

248
00:14:25.655 --> 00:14:29.305
Uncompensated exceptions go
to the authorizing official.

249
00:14:29.305 --> 00:14:32.180
And the repository becomes the only path.

250
00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:35.330
Write the policy down,
test it, prove it ran.

251
00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:39.330
Book 04 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

252
00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:40.160
Thank you.
